<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catholic Church</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Roman Catholic Church, Ecclesia Catholica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Catholic Church is the largest Christian denomination, made up of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> and 23 Eastern Catholic Churches organised into nearly 3,500 dioceses and eparchies. Its doctrine rests on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a> and holds that its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> succeed the apostles and that the pope, the bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, succeeds Saint Peter. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>, celebrated in the Mass, stands at the centre of its seven sacraments. The church had a large role in the development of Western civilisation; it separated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> after 431, from the Oriental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> after 451 and gradually from the Eastern Orthodox, and the 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> prompted its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catholic-church.jpg" alt="Catholic Church" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jebulon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Peter's_Basilica_facade,_Rome,_Italy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Catholic, from the Greek katholikos, 'universal', is first attested as an adjective for the church in the early second century. The first known use of the phrase &quot;the catholic church&quot; is in a letter written around 100 CE by Ignatius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> to the Smyrnaeans. In 380 the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> issued the edict De fide catolica when he established the state church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>The church teaches that its public ministry began at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentecost">Pentecost</a>, fifty days after the day on which Christ is believed to have risen, when the apostles are held to have received the Holy Spirit. Because Christians refused to join pagan festivals and civic rituals, some authorities feared they were angering the gods, and the resulting prosecutions lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was legalised in the 4th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The church separated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> after the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in 431 and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-orthodox-churches">Oriental Orthodox Churches</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451. Its separation from the Eastern Orthodox churches developed gradually, with the Great Schism of 1054, and was solidified by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, amid disputes over papal authority.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-discovery">Age of Discovery</a>, Spanish, Portuguese and French explorers, conquistadors and missionaries spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and Oceania. Pope Alexander VI awarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over most of the new lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal, later confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a> of the 16th century prompted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. In 1798 the French general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-alexandre-berthier">Louis-Alexandre Berthier</a> imprisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vi">Pope Pius VI</a>, who died in captivity, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> re-established the church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> through the Concordat of 1801. In the 1960s Pope John XXIII convened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-vatican-council">Second Vatican Council</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The church follows an episcopal polity with three levels of clergy: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, who hold jurisdiction over a diocese or eparchy, priests, and deacons. It is led by the bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the pope, whose jurisdiction is called the Holy See and who is served by the Roman Curia. Of its 24 autonomous churches, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> is the largest. The church teaches that Christ instituted seven sacraments, among them baptism, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a> and penance.</p><p>It is the largest non-governmental provider of education and medical services in the world, and in 2010 its Pontifical Council for Pastoral Assistance to Health Care Workers said it managed 26 per cent of health care facilities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a>'s encyclical Rerum novarum of 1891 laid the foundations of its social teaching.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Catholic Church” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Russification bore on religion as well as language: the <b>Catholic Church</b> was persecuted, most monasteries were closed and nationalised, papal bulls could not be read without the government's consent, and in 1875 the Uniate Eparchy of Chełm-Belz was united with the Russian Orthodox Church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The uprising expropriated the Polish magnates, nobles and tenants: their lands, livestock and property passed to Cossacks, peasants, burghers and the state, the folwark manor system and <b>Catholic Church</b> holdings were eliminated after the Battle of Batih in 1652, and the peasants became free.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Holy Roman Empire and the <b>Catholic Church</b> banned Albert for his Protestantism, and the Teutonic Knights under Walter von Cronberg went on claiming Prussia without success.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was dominated by a landed nobility, many of whom served the dukes as officers and officials, alongside a clergy loyal to the <b>Catholic Church</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Catholic Church</b>, whose part in national liberation the constitution acknowledges, has strong influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/east-timor">East Timor · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Catholic Church</b> owned large properties and held great influence over the poor, which made liberal attacks on clerical privilege, tithes and the religious orders a source of fierce popular resentment.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
